      "text": "To find the volume of the frustum, we first need to calculate the volume of the original pyramid and the smaller pyramid, and then subtract the volume of the smaller pyramid from the volume of the original pyramid.\n\nThe volume \\( V \\) of a pyramid is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\times B \\times h \\]\n\nwhere \\( B \\) is the area of the base and \\( h \\) is the height (altitude) of the pyramid.\n\nFor the original pyramid with a base edge of 12 cm and an altitude of 8 cm:\n\n\\[ B = 12^2 = 144 \\, \\text{cm}^2 \\]\n\\[ h = 8 \\, \\text{cm} \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the original pyramid is:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 144 \\times 8 = 384 \\, \\text{cm}^3 \\]\n\nFor the smaller pyramid with a base edge of 6 cm and an altitude of 4 cm:\n\n\\[ B = 6^2 = 36 \\, \\text{cm}^2 \\]\n\\[ h = 4 \\, \\text{cm} \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the smaller pyramid is:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{smaller}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 36 \\times 4 = 48 \\, \\text{cm}^3 \\]\n\nNow, to find the volume of the frustum, we subtract the volume of the smaller pyramid from the volume of the original pyramid:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{frustum}} = V_{\\text{original}} - V_{\\text{smaller}} = 384 - 48 = 336 \\, \\text{cm}^3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the volume of the frustum is \\(\\boxed{336}\\) cubic centimeters.",
